{"reference":"Amos 9","book":"Amos","chapter":9,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Amos 9 brings the book’s fifth and final vision to its climax: the Lord stands by or over the altar, the sanctuary collapses, and no hiding place prevents judgment. The chapter then turns to rebuilding David’s fallen booth and to restored cities, vineyards, and land. The received book presents these movements together, though the origin and precise relationship of the restoration ending to the preceding judgment remain debated.","verse_refs":["Amos 9:1-10","Amos 9:1-4","Amos 9:11-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_009_4f71ba2801b95b58711d87ba"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions in the Chapter","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verse 7 compares Israel’s exodus with the movements of the Philistines and Arameans. The comparison does not deny Israel’s story; rather, it challenges any exclusive claim on divine governance. Verses 11–15 likewise belong to the chapter as received, but proposals about when and how this restoration passage took shape remain open.","verse_refs":["Amos 9:11-12","Amos 9:11-15","Amos 9:7-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_009_6dad6394a0901544535bfe1e"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The “fallen booth of David” in verse 11 is not defined further. It may refer to a dynasty, a damaged polity, territorial rule, or a broader restored people. Later messianic and ecclesial readings are important reception histories, but they do not settle Amos’s sole intended referent. The chapter’s land-restoration language should also be distinguished from attempts to use it as a direct warrant for modern nationalism, displacement, or violence.","verse_refs":["Amos 9:11-12","Amos 9:11-15","Amos 9:7-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_009_6dad6394a0901544535bfe1e"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Text and Transmission","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Amos survives in related but sometimes divergent Hebrew and Greek textual forms. Early Hebrew manuscripts, including 4Q78 and 4Q82, preserve substantial portions of Amos, but they do not resolve every difficulty. This is especially relevant to verses 11–12: Acts 15 quotes a Greek form that speaks of a remnant of humanity, whereas the Masoretic form speaks of possessing the remnant of Edom. The difference should be named rather than treated as a careless quotation.","verse_refs":["Amos 9:1-10","Amos 9:1-15","Amos 9:11-15","Amos 9:5-15","Amos 9:5-6","Amos 9:11-12","Amos 9:7-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_009_50ff19006730e46efd399718","reader_path_amos_009_f8435812cd82bec59f78db49"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The order of the Twelve differs between textual traditions: the Masoretic sequence places Obadiah after Amos and before Jonah, while the Old Greek sequence places Abdias after Joel and before Jonah. Therefore, the mere placement of neighboring books does not by itself establish an editorial connection between them.","verse_refs":["Amos 9:12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_009_b701f8c8b07dfd4803783953"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why It Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The chapter’s severe judgment language calls for lament and ethical scrutiny; it does not authorize readers to enact vengeance, war, coercion, or punishment in the prophet’s name. Likewise, Christian use of Amos 9 in Acts should not erase Israel, Jewish readers, or continuing Jewish interpretation. Gentile inclusion is not evidence that the church replaces the Jewish people.","verse_refs":["Amos 9:1-10","Amos 9:1-15","Amos 9:11-12","Amos 9:11-15","Amos 9:7-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_009_d09892225f78df9baf0f03fb"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}